WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.938
Threat hunting is a proactive,

00:00:01.938 --> 00:00:06.716
analyst-driven search for adversary activity that may not have produced a clear alert.

00:00:06.716 --> 00:00:10.024
A hunter starts from a defensive question, a hypothesis,

00:00:10.024 --> 00:00:14.802
or a known behavior pattern and then looks for evidence in available telemetry.

00:00:14.802 --> 00:00:18.477
The work is structured investigation, not casual browsing through logs.

00:00:18.477 --> 00:00:22.520
It is also not a promise that something malicious is present.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:27.299
A hunt can confirm suspicious activity, reject a weak idea, reveal telemetry gaps,

00:00:27.299 --> 00:00:29.136
or produce a better detection.

00:00:29.136 --> 00:00:31.709
Threat hunting is different from alert triage.

00:00:31.709 --> 00:00:35.017
In triage, the system has already created an alert,

00:00:35.017 --> 00:00:39.060
and the analyst decides whether it is benign, suspicious, or incident-worthy.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:39.795
In hunting,

00:00:39.795 --> 00:00:44.206
the analyst is looking for activity that existing alerts may have missed

00:00:44.206 --> 00:00:46.044
or did not classify clearly.

00:00:46.044 --> 00:00:48.616
Hunting is also different from incident response.

00:00:48.616 --> 00:00:53.027
Incident response begins when there is enough evidence to treat the situation

00:00:53.027 --> 00:00:55.232
as a possible or confirmed incident.

00:00:55.232 --> 00:01:00.378
Hunting may feed incident response, but it usually begins earlier and with more uncertainty.

00:01:00.378 --> 00:01:02.216
Hunting is not vulnerability scanning.

00:01:02.216 --> 00:01:05.156
A scanner asks whether assets have known weaknesses.

00:01:05.156 --> 00:01:09.934
A hunt asks whether behavior in the environment suggests suspicious or adversary activity.

00:01:09.934 --> 00:01:12.140
Hunting is not compliance review either.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:15.080
Compliance work may check whether required controls exist,

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.123
while hunting tests operational visibility and asks what the telemetry shows.

00:01:19.123 --> 00:01:22.798
A good security program can include all of these activities,

00:01:22.798 --> 00:01:24.636
but they answer different questions.

00:01:24.636 --> 00:01:29.047
In a SOC, hunting fits between detection engineering, alert operations, threat intelligence,

00:01:29.047 --> 00:01:30.149
and incident response.

00:01:30.149 --> 00:01:34.560
Hunters use telemetry from endpoints, identity systems, DNS, cloud logs, proxy logs,

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:35.663
and other sources.

00:01:35.663 --> 00:01:39.706
They may use ATT&CK-style behavior mapping, recent incident lessons, business context,

00:01:39.706 --> 00:01:43.014
or known environmental risks to decide where to look.

00:01:43.014 --> 00:01:45.587
When a hunt produces a useful pattern,

00:01:45.587 --> 00:01:48.527
detection engineers can turn it into repeatable logic.

00:01:48.527 --> 00:01:50.732
When a hunt finds suspicious evidence,

00:01:50.732 --> 00:01:54.040
incident responders can take over with an escalation process.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:55.510
The defensive discipline matters.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:59.553
A hunt should have authorization, scope, notes, and evidence handling expectations.

00:01:59.553 --> 00:02:03.229
It should define the environment, time window, data sources, assumptions,

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:04.331
and decision criteria.

00:02:04.331 --> 00:02:05.802
Hunters should avoid overclaiming.

00:02:05.802 --> 00:02:07.639
Unusual is not automatically malicious,

00:02:07.639 --> 00:02:11.315
and absence of evidence is not proof that nothing happened.

00:02:11.315 --> 00:02:15.726
A practical hunt produces a documented outcome: evidence found, evidence not found,

00:02:15.726 --> 00:02:20.871
a refined hypothesis, a telemetry gap, a tuned alert, or a new detection idea.

00:02:20.871 --> 00:02:26.017
That is what makes hunting valuable as an engineering practice rather than random searching.

00:02:26.017 --> 00:02:31.163
A useful way to evaluate a hunt is to ask what decision it supports.

00:02:31.163 --> 00:02:35.573
The decision might be to escalate, close the hypothesis, improve a detection,

00:02:35.573 --> 00:02:38.514
request better logging, or schedule a follow-up hunt.

00:02:38.514 --> 00:02:42.189
That decision focus keeps the work from becoming open-ended exploration.

00:02:42.189 --> 00:02:46.600
It also helps managers and other analysts understand why the hunt was

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.540
worth doing even if no incident was found.
